# `just template ...` -- scaffold + manage repo-local command groups
# (ADR-00000010). The `template` namespace, reached via the consumer entry's
# `mod? template`.

# Default: bare `just template` prints help (the `default` recipe must come
# first -- a module otherwise treats the first recipe, `new`, as default and
# errors because it needs an argument). `default` delegates to `help` so the
# usage text has a single source.
default: help

# `help` is a named twin of `default` reachable as `just template help` (and
# `just template h`), since the dashed `just template --help` cannot be a just
# recipe/alias -- with a `help` recipe present just then hints "Did you mean
# 'help'?" instead of a bare error. Bare `just template` prints the same via
# `default`.
alias h := help

# just template help (or `just template h`) -> language-aware recipe listing:
# translated one-line summaries via the shared `_msg help` i18n renderer
# (honours $LANG / --lang). Bare `just template` prints the same via `default`.
help *args:
    @"$(dirname "$(readlink -f '{{source_file()}}')")/../docker/lib/help.sh" template {{args}}

# Scaffold a new repo-local command group: create script/local/<name>/ from
# the skeleton and register it in script/local/justfile.local.
#
# `cd {{justfile_directory}}` lands at the repo root (the entry's dir,
# reliable whether reached via `--working-directory` or a bare invocation;
# `[no-cd]` / `invocation_directory` are not, so the scaffolder runs from
# a known-good cwd) before running new.sh, which writes to ./script/local/.
#
# `{{quote(name)}}`, and NOT a hand-written quote around it. An
# interpolation is TEXT handed to the recipe's own shell, so the bare form
# re-split it: `just template new 'two words'` reached new.sh as two
# arguments, and new.sh -- which validates `^[a-z][a-z0-9_-]*$`, and so
# would have refused the real name -- was handed `words`, created that
# group, registered it in script/local/justfile.local and exited 0
# (base#1299).
#
# Measured against a recorder that prints its argument count and then one
# line per argument, for each of the three forms a reviewer reaches for:
#
#   name          {{name}}         '{{name}}'           {{quote(name)}}
#   two words     2 args           1 arg                1 arg
#   foo|bar       PIPELINE, 127    1 arg                1 arg
#   two'words     SYNTAX ERROR     SYNTAX ERROR         1 arg
#   a''b          1 arg `ab`       1 arg `ab`           1 arg `a''b`
#   ' --help #    SYNTAX ERROR     2 args: `` `--help`  1 arg
#   $USER         1 arg `<user>`   1 arg, literal       1 arg, literal
#   deploy-aws    1 arg            1 arg                1 arg
#   (empty)       0 args           1 empty arg          1 empty arg
#
# The `' --help #` row is why the hand-written quote is not enough: it
# closes the quote the recipe opened and hands new.sh a FLAG, which that
# script answers by printing its help and exiting 0. `quote()` is just's
# own shell-escaper -- it wraps in single quotes and escapes embedded
# apostrophes -- and is the only form right for every input.
#
# A fourth form, `"{{name}}"`, fixes the re-split and is wrong for its
# own reason: it EXPANDS, so `$USER` arrives as the operator's name,
# which satisfies the validator and scaffolds a group named after them.
new name:
    @cd {{justfile_directory()}} && script/template/new.sh {{quote(name)}}
